- the 24-hour telephone desk, including the language line;
- the online claim portal and the Northline mobile application;
- email to the published claims address, which is monitored on business days;
- the appointed agent, as provided in Section 5.4;
- postal mail and overnight courier;
- a lawsuit or attorney letter received by any Northline office, Legal, or the registered agent.

- a death, or an injury described as life-threatening;
- a child under 18 taken to a hospital;
- a dwelling that is not safe to occupy, including no heat in freezing weather, an open roof, fire damage, or a sewage backup that has made the home unusable;
- a commercial insured whose operations are stopped;
- a caller who says they are in danger, or who describes a loss caused by a person who is still at the property;
- any report the call-taker believes involves a vulnerable adult who may not be able to protect their own interest.

- the claim number and date of loss;
- the policy number and a plain description of the coverage part under review, without stating that coverage has been accepted;
- the name, telephone number, and email of the assigned adjuster, or of the desk if assignment is not complete;
- a list of information still needed, written as specific items ("photos of the ceiling and the failed supply line") rather than "send all relevant documents";
- a statement that the insured may submit information at any time and that Northline will consider it;
- the fraud warning required by the state where the loss occurred or the policy was issued, as specified in the state addendum;
- for property losses, a short note that temporary reasonable repairs to protect the property may be made, that receipts should be kept, and that permanent repairs should wait for inspection unless delay would increase the damage.

- refusing to open a file when the caller describes a past event and a possible Northline policy;
- closing an unmatched notice as "no policy" without a second search by another person;
- telling a caller that notice is too late to submit;
- any commitment of make-safe spend above the associate's published limit;
- contact with the media about a reported loss.

- policy number, form numbers, and edition dates relied on;
- date of loss and, if different, discovery date;
- persons seeking coverage and the basis for each;
- facts relied on, each tied to a source (inspection, photo, witness, pleading, expert);
- insuring-agreement conclusion;
- each exclusion or condition considered and the conclusion, including those considered and rejected, so the file shows the work;
- deductible and limit arithmetic;
- other insurance;
- result: covered, partially covered, not covered, or facts insufficient;
- if facts are insufficient, the specific missing items and the diary date;
- citations to any state-addendum rule that changes the reading.

- the decisions the file still needs (cause, scope, injury, liability, identity of insured);
- the evidence that will support those decisions;
- who will collect it and by when;
- any evidence at risk of disappearing, and the preservation step.

- the amount payable is at or below the desk-authority threshold published in the current payment bulletin, the photos and invoices are enough to measure the loss, and the cause is not in real dispute;
- the property has been demolished or the vehicle sold, and the file explains what secondary evidence was used;
- a catastrophe bulletin under CLM-010 authorizes desk adjustment for that peril and geography, and the file meets the bulletin's photo standard;
- the insured refuses access after two written requests, in which case the file proceeds on the evidence available and records the refusal for a cooperation analysis.

- the coverage screen supports payment of the items being paid;
- the estimate or evaluation supports the dollar amount, after deductible, depreciation, limits, and comparative fault as applicable;
- the payees have been verified against the policy, the mortgagee clause, any lien the file has recognized, and any representation agreement;
- the approver has authority for the occurrence;
- the reserve is at least equal to the payment plus what is still expected, or it is increased in the same session under CLM-004;
- no legal hold, SIU hold, or bankruptcy hold is open on the file. An SIU hold follows CLM-007 and is not improvised by the adjuster.

- the issued policy image, including endorsements;
- a coverage screen under CLM-002 that states the result "not covered" or "partially covered," with facts tied to sources;
- the investigation steps that the screen relies on, completed or explicitly determined to be unnecessary;
- for a condition-based denial, the prejudice analysis the state requires;
- for a fraud-based denial, the SIU conclusion required by CLM-007;
- the draft letter;
- the approval required by Section 8.

- the date, claim number, policy number, and date of loss;
- a plain statement that the claim, or the identified part of it, is denied;
- the facts Northline relies on, written so the customer can recognize their own loss;
- the policy language relied on, quoted accurately, with the form number and edition;
- the reasoning that connects the facts to that language, in short paragraphs rather than a string of citations;
- any portion that is accepted and will be paid, with the payment timing;
- what the customer may send if they believe a fact is wrong, and the address or portal method for sending it;
- the right to an internal appeal under CLM-009, including the deadline;
- any appraisal, mediation, or department-of-insurance information the state addendum requires;
- the name and direct telephone number of a person who can explain the letter.

- defense of a suit in which some allegations would be covered and some would not;
- a large investigation that will continue past thirty days with a live exclusion question;
- payment of make-safe costs before the screen is finished, where the customer could reasonably think the payment accepted full coverage;
- a late-notice file on which the Company is still investigating and prejudice is not yet known;
- an additional-insured tender the Company will defend while it examines the endorsement's trigger.

- who, other than the insured, may have caused or contributed to the loss;
- what evidence identifies them (contract, police report, product, work order);
- whether the policy preserves subrogation and whether any waiver-of-subrogation endorsement applies;
- whether another insurer has been identified under CLM-002;
- whether damaged property will have salvage value if Northline pays a total loss;
- what evidence must be preserved now.

- the expected net recovery is below the threshold and no deductible-return purpose justifies a limited demand;
- liability evidence is insufficient and further investigation is unlikely to repair it;
- the defendant cannot pay and no insurer responds, after a reasonable asset or coverage check proportionate to the amount;
- the insured's defense or ongoing relationship under a waiver endorsement would be harmed;
- Legal advises that the theory is not viable in the jurisdiction.

- the date received and the channel;
- the claim number and the decision or conduct complained of;
- a short statement of what the customer wants;
- any deadline, especially a regulator's deadline;
- the person assigned to respond;
- whether the issue is service, coverage, value, payment, or conduct.

- set acknowledgment and inspection times that differ from CLM-001 and CLM-003, within the law;
- authorize desk adjustment for specified perils and size bands if the photo standard in the bulletin is met;
- authorize a make-safe advance up to a stated dollar amount before inspection;
- authorize a temporary price-list adjustment when local labor or materials have genuinely moved, with an end date;
- name the vendor panel, the firms allowed to tarp or extract, and the fee schedule;
- set lodging, mileage, and authority for deployed adjusters;
- assign extra denial approvers under CLM-006 without lowering the content of a denial;
- extend SIU's first-assessment time under CLM-007, without allowing a silent payment hold.

- instruct staff to pay flood on a policy that excludes flood;
- waive mortgagee payees, minor-payee rules, or proof of loss where the Company intends to rely on the condition;
- permit secret recordings, pretext investigations, or a lowering of reserves to manage the financial result;
- close appeals or lengthen a statutory complaint deadline;
- authorize off-panel vendors except through the written exception in Section 9.
